---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Chart from "../components/Chart.astro";
import { groupCpi, regional, realWages, householdIncome, asOf } from "../lib/data";
import { pct, number, asOfLabel, myr } from "../lib/format";

const groups = groupCpi();
const rw = realWages();
const hh = householdIncome();
const reg = regional();

const cpiAsOf = asOf(["cpi_national.json", "cpi_state.json"]);
const wagesAsOf = asOf(["real_wages.json"]);
const incomeAsOf = asOf(["household_income.json"]);

// --- Finding 1: real incomes rose, unevenly -----------------------------------------
const wageSpread = reg.spreads.wages.real_growth;
const incomeSpread = reg.spreads.income.real_growth;

// National real wage growth. Read from the artifact's dedicated `national` block: the
// state-keyed `series` has no Malaysia row, because it inner-joins against the state CPI,
// which carries only the 16 states.
const nationalWages = rw.national.slice().sort((a, b) => a.year - b.year);
const w0 = nationalWages[0];
const w1 = nationalWages.at(-1);
const nationalNominalGrowth =
  w0 && w1 && w0.wages_median && w1.wages_median
    ? (w1.wages_median / w0.wages_median - 1) * 100
    : null;
const nationalRealGrowth =
  w0 && w1 && w0.real_median && w1.real_median
    ? (w1.real_median / w0.real_median - 1) * 100
    : null;
const nationalInflation =
  w0 && w1 && w0.cpi && w1.cpi ? (w1.cpi / w0.cpi - 1) * 100 : null;

// --- Finding 2: the divergence is on the income side --------------------------------
const wageInflationSpread = reg.spreads.wages.inflation.spread_pp ?? 0;
const wageRealSpread = reg.spreads.wages.real_growth.spread_pp ?? 0;

// --- Finding 3: food away vs food at home -------------------------------------------
const foodAway = groups.meta.cumulative_from_base_pct?.["001"] ?? null;
const foodHome = groups.meta.cumulative_from_base_pct?.["011"] ?? null;

const validation = rw.meta.validation_against_dosm;

// National household income growth, computed rather than pasted in. The two survey years are
// 2012 and 2024; the ratio form is used because subtracting inflation from nominal growth
// drifts over a 12-year horizon.
const hhNational = hh.national.slice().sort((a, b) => a.year - b.year);
const h0 = hhNational[0];
const h1 = hhNational.at(-1);
const nationalIncomeGrowth =
  h0?.income_median && h1?.income_median ? (h1.income_median / h0.income_median - 1) * 100 : null;
const nationalIncomeRealGrowth =
  h0?.income_median && h1?.income_median && h0?.cpi && h1?.cpi
    ? ((h1.income_median / h0.income_median) / (h1.cpi / h0.cpi) - 1) * 100
    : null;

const descriptions = [
  {
    code: "001",
    label: "Food away from home",
    value: foodAway,
    colour: "var(--series-2)",
  },
  { code: "011", label: "Food at home", value: foodHome, colour: "var(--series-3)" },
];

const divergenceRows = [
  ...reg.wages_ranking.map((r) => ({
    state: r.state,
    cumulative_inflation_pct: r.cumulative_inflation_pct,
    real_growth_pct: r.real_wage_growth_pct,
    measure: "Wages",
  })),
  ...reg.income_ranking.map((r) => ({
    state: r.state,
    cumulative_inflation_pct: r.cumulative_inflation_pct,
    real_growth_pct: r.real_income_growth_pct,
    measure: "Household income",
  })),
];
---
<Base
  title="Malaysia Cost-of-Living Reality Check"
  description="Is purchasing power in Malaysia actually improving? Real income, inflation and regional divergence, built entirely from DOSM open data."
>
  <h1>Is purchasing power in Malaysia actually improving?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Short answer: yes on average, but the national average hides a spread between states that
    is two to three times wider than any difference in prices. Here is what the data supports —
    and where it refuses to support the usual story.
  </p>

  <div class="grid" style="margin:1.5rem 0">
    <div class="stat">
      <p class="label">National real median wage</p>
      <p class="value">{pct(nationalRealGrowth)}</p>
      <p class="note">
        {w0?.year}–{w1?.year}, in 2010 ringgit. Wages rose {pct(nationalNominalGrowth)} nominal
        against {pct(nationalInflation, 1, false)} inflation. Household income, a different
        measure: {pct(nationalIncomeGrowth)} nominal.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <p class="label">Spread across states</p>
      <p class="value">{number(wageRealSpread, 1)} pp</p>
      <p class="note">
        Real wage growth ranges from {pct(wageSpread.min_pct)} in {wageSpread.min_state} to{" "}
        {pct(wageSpread.max_pct)} in {wageSpread.max_state}.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <p class="label">Spread in prices</p>
      <p class="value">{number(wageInflationSpread, 1)} pp</p>
      <p class="note">
        Cumulative inflation across states is far more uniform than income growth. Prices are
        not what is diverging.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Finding 1 — Real incomes rose, but the map is deeply uneven</h2>
  <p>
    Two independent sources agree on the shape. The employer wage survey (individual employees)
    and the Household Income and Expenditure Survey (households) start in different years and
    measure different things, but both show a substantial national gain and a very wide spread.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <caption>Real income growth by state, both sources</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Measure</th>
          <th scope="col">Period</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">National</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Lowest</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Highest</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">Spread</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Median wage</th>
          <td>{rw.meta.period.start_year}–{rw.meta.period.end_year}</td>
          <td class="num">{pct(nationalRealGrowth)}</td>
          <td class="num">{pct(wageSpread.min_pct)} <small>({wageSpread.min_state})</small></td>
          <td class="num">{pct(wageSpread.max_pct)} <small>({wageSpread.max_state})</small></td>
          <td class="num">{number(wageSpread.spread_pp, 1)} pp</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Household median income</th>
          <td>{hh.meta.period.start_year}–{hh.meta.period.end_year}</td>
          <td class="num">{pct(nationalIncomeRealGrowth)}</td>
          <td class="num">{pct(incomeSpread.min_pct)} <small>({incomeSpread.min_state})</small></td>
          <td class="num">{pct(incomeSpread.max_pct)} <small>({incomeSpread.max_state})</small></td>
          <td class="num">{number(incomeSpread.spread_pp, 1)} pp</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <h3>Read this before trusting the medians</h3>
    <p>
      The wage figures are the median <em>of the employee population</em>. That median moves
      when the mix of employees changes — more part-time work, more or fewer non-citizens in
      the workforce — with no change in anyone's pay. It is a real caveat we cannot correct
      without microdata. Employment surveys also exclude the self-employed and informal sector
      entirely.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Finding 2 — The divergence is about wages, not prices</h2>
  <p>
    If cost of living were diverging because some states got expensive, prices would be the
    thing that spread out. They are not.
  </p>

  <Chart
    name="divergence-scatter"
    props={{ rows: divergenceRows }}
    title="Price inflation versus real income growth, by state"
    subtitle="Each dot is one state. Flat and wide means prices are similar everywhere while income growth is not."
    height={380}
  />

  <p>
    Cumulative inflation across the 16 states spans {number(wageInflationSpread, 1)} percentage
    points. Real income growth spans {number(wageRealSpread, 1)}. The states that fell behind
    did so on the income side. W.P. Putrajaya is the clearest case: it has both the highest
    cumulative inflation and the lowest real household income growth.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>No causal claim.</strong> The data says the divergence is on the income side, not
      the price side. It does not say why. Explaining that would need data this project does not
      have, and we would rather leave the question open than invent a narrative.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Finding 3 — Your spending pattern matters more than your postcode</h2>
  <p>
    Eating out and cooking at home are separate categories in DOSM's CPI, and they have moved
    very differently.
  </p>

  <Chart
    name="group-comparison"
    props={{
      periods: groups.periods,
      series: groups.series,
      names: groups.meta.group_names,
      codes: ["001", "011"],
    }}
    title="Food away from home versus food at home"
    subtitle="Cumulative price change since 2010, national"
    height={330}
  />

  <div class="grid" style="margin:1.25rem 0">
    {
      descriptions.map((d) => (
        <div class="stat">
          <p class="label">{d.label}</p>
          <p class="value" style={`color:${d.colour}`}>
            {d.value === null ? "no data" : `+${d.value.toFixed(1)}%`}
          </p>
          <p class="note">since 2010</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p>
    A household that eats out regularly has experienced meaningfully more inflation on that
    part of its budget than one that cooks. That is a difference in <em>what you buy</em>, not
    in <em>where you live</em> — and you can
    <a href="/calculator">work out your own rate</a> from your own spending split.
  </p>

  <p>
    And two results that cut against the expected narrative. Over 2010–2025, low-income
    households' CPI rose <strong>35.0%</strong> against a headline <strong>34.6%</strong> — a
    gap of 0.4 percentage points, essentially nothing. Urban households, meanwhile, saw
    <strong>35.6%</strong> against <strong>29.5%</strong> for rural. Where you live (urban vs
    rural) moved inflation about fifteen times more than how much you earn did.
  </p>

  <div class="callout good">
    <p>
      <strong>We report that as found.</strong> "Inflation hits the poor hardest" is a
      reasonable prior and it is not what this data shows, at the aggregate level. The
      <em>composition</em> does differ — low-income inflation ran hotter on restaurants and
      tobacco and cooler on education and food — but the total does not. We would rather say
      so than pick a period that makes it look bigger.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><a href="/income">Income</a> — nominal versus real, by state, from both sources</li>
    <li><a href="/states">States</a> — the full ranking and the divergence in detail</li>
    <li><a href="/calculator">Your basket</a> — your own inflation rate from your spending split</li>
    <li><a href="/forecast">Forecast</a> — 12 months ahead, including where the model fails</li>
    <li><a href="/prices">Prices</a> — what specific items actually cost, by state</li>
    <li><a href="/methodology">Methodology</a> — every formula, assumption, and limitation</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="data-note">
    <p>
      CPI as of {asOfLabel(cpiAsOf)} · wages as of {asOfLabel(wagesAsOf)} · household income as
      of {asOfLabel(incomeAsOf)}
    </p>
    <p>
      Deflation method validated against DOSM's own published real-wage series: {validation.n_compared}
      {" "}states compared, maximum difference {validation.max_abs_diff_median.toFixed(2)} RM. This
      is asserted by a test, not merely claimed.
    </p>
    <p>
      National median wage {w0?.year}: {myr(w0?.wages_median)} nominal, {myr(w0?.real_median)} in
      2010 ringgit → {w1?.year}: {myr(w1?.wages_median)} nominal, {myr(w1?.real_median)} real.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
